<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A2.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure A6</span>: </span><span id="A2.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Extended results of <a href="#S5.F3" title="In Evaluation metrics. ‣ 5.1 Experimental Settings ‣ 5 Synthetic Experiments ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Distortion factor <math id="A2.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(x)=u_{M}(x)/N_{M}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(x)=u_{M}(x)/N_{M}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> vs. unnormalized target density <math id="A2.F6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}(p_{1}(x),p_{2}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}(p_{1}(x),p_{2}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> on the 2D grid domain. Red points indicate outliers, defined as states where <math id="A2.F6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(x)</annotation></semantics></math> falls outside <math id="A2.F6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[Q_{1}-1.5\cdot\mathrm{IQR},Q_{3}+1.5\cdot\mathrm{IQR}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>IQR</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>IQR</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[Q_{1}-1.5\cdot\mathrm{IQR},Q_{3}+1.5\cdot\mathrm{IQR}]</annotation></semantics></math> (gray dotted lines), where <math id="A2.F6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.F6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> are the 25th/75th percentiles and <math id="A2.F6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{IQR}=Q_{3}-Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>IQR</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{IQR}=Q_{3}-Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The green dashed line marks <math id="A2.F6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/Z_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/Z_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.F6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{M}=\sum_{x}\mathcal{G}(p_{1}(x),p_{2}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{M}=\sum_{x}\mathcal{G}(p_{1}(x),p_{2}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Real-world Experiments</h3>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.1 </span>Fragment-based Molecule Generation</h4>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward functions.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use three reward functions:
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SEH</span> is the predicted binding affinity to soluble epoxide hydrolase, computed using a pre-trained proxy model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, with raw values divided by 8 to normalize to approximately <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>;
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SA</span> is the synthetic accessibility score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, computed using RDKit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and transformed as <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(10-\text{SA}_{\text{raw}})/9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>SA</mtext><mtext>raw</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(10-\text{SA}_{\text{raw}})/9</annotation></semantics></math> to map to <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with higher values indicating easier synthesis; and
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QED</span> is the quantitative estimate of drug-likeness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, computed using RDKit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, already in <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model details and hyperparameters.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All GFlowNets use a Graph Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> backbone with 6 layers and embedding dimension 128, taking a junction tree representation as input where nodes correspond to fragments and edges encode attachment points.
Training uses the Sub-Trajectory Balance (SubTB; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>) objective with Adam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> (learning rate <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>), batch size 64, sampling temperature <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.99</annotation></semantics></math>, random action probability 0.05, and 15,000 iterations.
For single-objective GFlowNets, we use <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in\{32,64\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in\{32,64\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For multi-objective baselines (MOGFN and HN-GFN), preferences <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}\in\Delta^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}\in\Delta^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled from <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Dirichlet}(\alpha=1.0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Dirichlet</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Dirichlet}(\alpha=1.0)</annotation></semantics></math> during training, with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=64</annotation></semantics></math>.
MOGFN conditions the model by concatenating preferences to the graph-level embedding before the output heads.
HN-GFN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> uses a HyperNetwork with a 3-layer MLP (hidden dimension 100) to generate policy network weights from preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the classifier-guided baseline, we train a 3-way joint classifier <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y_{1},y_{2},y_{3}\mid s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y_{1},y_{2},y_{3}\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math> following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garipov et al. [10]</a></cite>.
The classifier is a Graph Transformer with 4 layers, 2 attention heads, and embedding dimension 128, outputting a <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> joint probability tensor.
We condition on a terminal indicator and train for 15,000 iterations using Adam (learning rate <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size 8 per distribution).
An EMA target network (smoothing 0.995) provides stable targets, with non-terminal loss weight <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> linearly increasing from 0 to 1 over the first 4,000 steps.
Training samples trajectories from pre-trained single-objective GFlowNets (SEH, SA, QED with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=32</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mixing reward-sharpened GFlowNets for scalarization.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the scalarization target <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{M}^{*}(x)\propto(\sum_{i}w_{i}R_{i}(x))^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{M}^{*}(x)\propto(\sum_{i}w_{i}R_{i}(x))^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we leverage pre-trained ingredient GFlowNets trained on <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>β</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. In our mixing framework (<a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Mixture of GFlowNets ‣ 4 Method ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), sampling from <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{M}^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{M}^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> requires ingredient models with terminating distributions <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}(x)\propto R_{i}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}(x)\propto R_{i}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which we can then mix as <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{M,F}\propto(\sum_{i}w_{i}Z_{i}u_{i}p_{i,F})^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{M,F}\propto(\sum_{i}w_{i}Z_{i}u_{i}p_{i,F})^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, our ingredient GFlowNets are trained with reward exponent <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, inducing terminating distributions <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{i}(x)\propto R_{i}(x)^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{i}(x)\propto R_{i}(x)^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> with partition functions <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Z}_{i}=\sum_{x}R_{i}(x)^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Z}_{i}=\sum_{x}R_{i}(x)^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The unnormalized reward can be recovered as <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}(x)=(\tilde{Z}_{i}\,\tilde{p}_{i}(x))^{1/\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}(x)=(\tilde{Z}_{i}\,\tilde{p}_{i}(x))^{1/\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{Z}_{i}\tilde{p}_{i}(x)=R_{i}(x)^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{Z}_{i}\tilde{p}_{i}(x)=R_{i}(x)^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting into the scalarization target:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{M}^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{M}^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}w_{i}\,\bigl(\tilde{Z}_{i}\,\tilde{p}_{i}(x)\bigr)^{1/\beta}\right)^{\beta}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}w_{i}\,\bigl(\tilde{Z}_{i}\,\tilde{p}_{i}(x)\bigr)^{1/\beta}\right)^{\beta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The mixing policy is therefore:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{p}_{M,F}(s^{\prime}\mid s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{p}_{M,F}(s^{\prime}\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}w_{i}\,\bigl(\tilde{Z}_{i}\tilde{u}_{i}(s)\cdot\tilde{p}_{i,F}(s^{\prime}\mid s)\bigr)^{1/\beta}\right)^{\beta}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\left(\sum_{i=1}^{k}w_{i}\,\bigl(\tilde{Z}_{i}\tilde{u}_{i}(s)\cdot\tilde{p}_{i,F}(s^{\prime}\mid s)\bigr)^{1/\beta}\right)^{\beta},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which allows composing pre-trained reward-sharpened models without retraining for each preference vector <math id="A2.SS2.SSS1.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.2 </span>Atom-based Molecule Generation</h4>

<section id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reward functions.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use three reward functions:
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GAP</span> is the HOMO–LUMO gap predicted by an MXMNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> proxy trained on QM9, normalized using percentile-based scaling and clipped to <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,2]</annotation></semantics></math>;
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SA</span> is the synthetic accessibility score <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, computed using RDKit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and transformed as <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(10-\text{SA}_{\text{raw}})/9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>SA</mtext><mtext>raw</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(10-\text{SA}_{\text{raw}})/9</annotation></semantics></math> to map to <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>; and
<span id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QED</span> is the quantitative estimate of drug-likeness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, computed using RDKit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, already in <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model details and hyperparameters.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All GFlowNets use a Graph Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> backbone with 4 layers and embedding dimension 64, taking the current molecular graph as input with node features encoding atom types and edge features encoding bond types.
Training uses the Sub-Trajectory Balance (SubTB; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>) objective with Adam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> (learning rate <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math>), batch size 64, sampling temperature <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.99</annotation></semantics></math>, random action probability 0.05, and 15,000 iterations.
For single-objective GFlowNets, we use <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=32</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For multi-objective baselines (MOGFN and HN-GFN), preferences <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}\in\Delta^{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝎</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}\in\Delta^{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled from <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Dirichlet}(\alpha=1.0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Dirichlet</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Dirichlet}(\alpha=1.0)</annotation></semantics></math> during training, with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=32</annotation></semantics></math>.
MOGFN conditions the model by concatenating preferences to the graph-level embedding before the output heads.
HN-GFN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> uses a HyperNetwork with a 3-layer MLP (hidden dimension 100) to generate policy network weights from preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the classifier-guided baseline, we train a 3-way joint classifier <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y_{1},y_{2},y_{3}\mid s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y_{1},y_{2},y_{3}\mid s)</annotation></semantics></math> following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Garipov et al. [10]</a></cite>.
The classifier is a Graph Transformer with 4 layers, 2 attention heads, and embedding dimension 128, outputting a <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\times 3\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\times 3\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> joint probability tensor.
We condition on a terminal indicator and train for 15,000 iterations using Adam (learning rate <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, weight decay <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size 8 per distribution).
An EMA target network (smoothing 0.995) provides stable targets, with non-terminal loss weight <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> linearly increasing from 0 to 1 over the first 4,000 steps.
Training samples trajectories from pre-trained single-objective GFlowNets (GAP, SA, QED with <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=32</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mixing reward-sharpened GFlowNets for scalarization.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As in the fragment-based setting (<a href="#A2.SS2.SSS1" title="B.2.1 Fragment-based Molecule Generation ‣ B.2 Real-world Experiments ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2.1</span></a>), we leverage pre-trained ingredient GFlowNets trained on <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{i}^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>β</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math> to sample from the scalarization target <math id="A2.SS2.SSS2.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{M}^{*}(x)\propto(\sum_{i}w_{i}R_{i}(x))^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{M}^{*}(x)\propto(\sum_{i}w_{i}R_{i}(x))^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">B.2.3 </span>Additional Results</h4>

<div id="A2.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A2.T3" title="In B.2.3 Additional Results ‣ B.2 Real-world Experiments ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tables</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A3</span></a>, <a href="#A2.T4" title="Table A4 ‣ B.2.3 Additional Results ‣ B.2 Real-world Experiments ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a>, <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table A5 ‣ B.2.3 Additional Results ‣ B.2 Real-world Experiments ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a>, <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table A6 ‣ Effect of reward correlations. ‣ B.2.3 Additional Results ‣ B.2 Real-world Experiments ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A6</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure A7 ‣ Effect of reward correlations. ‣ B.2.3 Additional Results ‣ B.2 Real-world Experiments ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A7</span></a> show additional results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A2.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table A3</span>: </span><span id="A2.T3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Results of logical operators with our mixing policy on molecule generation tasks. We report the percentage of samples falling into each of 8 bins based on reward thresholds: Fragment-based (SEH: 0.5, SA: 0.6, QED: 0.25) and Atom-based (GAP: 0.85, SA: 0.4, QED: 0.4). <span id="A2.T3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> indicates the <span id="A2.T3.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">target bin</span>: for harmonic mean (<math id="A2.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math>), where all objectives are high; for contrast (<span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A2.T3.m4.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="8.06" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-0.81px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 8.06 8.06" width="8.06"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,8.06) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(4.03,0) translate(0,4.03)"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 0 L 0 -3.75 C 2.07 -3.75 3.75 -2.07 3.75 0 C 3.75 2.07 2.07 3.75 0 3.75 Z"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 M 3.75 0 C 3.75 2.07 2.07 3.75 0 3.75 C -2.07 3.75 -3.75 2.07 -3.75 0 C -3.75 -2.07 -2.07 -3.75 0 -3.75 C 2.07 -3.75 3.75 -2.07 3.75 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g></svg></span>), where only the first objective is high. Classifier-guided results are in <a href="#A2.T4" title="In B.2.3 Additional Results ‣ B.2 Real-world Experiments ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Tables</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A4</span></a> and <a href="#A2.T5" title="Table A5 ‣ B.2.3 Additional Results ‣ B.2 Real-world Experiments ‣ Appendix B Additional Experimental Details ‣ Training-free Composition of Pre-trained GFlowNets for Multi-Objective Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A5</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
